# Engineering OS Git Workflow

Language: communicate with humans in Spanish; all repository content (docs, comments, errors, config text) must be in English.

## Before any file change

1. Run `git status`. If there are uncommitted changes, STOP and notify the user.
2. Sync latest `main` (`fetch` + fast-forward). Never change files on `main`.
3. Create a task branch BEFORE editing anything:
   - `feat/<slug>`, `fix/<slug>`, `refactor/<slug>`, `docs/<slug>`, `chore/<slug>`, `test/<slug>`
4. Do all work only on that branch. One related task per branch.

## Before updating main

5. Review the full diff.
6. Run the relevant validations/tests for the change.
7. Create one Conventional Commit (no empty or process-only commits):
   - `feat(scope): ...`, `fix(scope): ...`, `docs(scope): ...`, `refactor(scope): ...`
   - Keep the human local Git identity as Author. Never set Author/Committer to Cursor.
   - Never change `git config`. End every agent commit message with:
     `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>`
8. Push the branch to GitHub.
9. Integrate the branch into `main`.
10. Push updated `main` to GitHub.
11. Verify `main` is clean and matches the expected commit.

`main` must always stay a coherent, working project state.

## Hard rules

- Never edit on `main`; never branch after changes already started.
- Never mix unrelated tasks; never force-push unless the user explicitly asks.
- Never overwrite existing local work; never delete work without an explicit reason.
- Touch only files required for the task.
- After finishing a task, stop; do not start the next one automatically.
- Human remains Git Author; agent credit is only via `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>`.

## Required completion report (Spanish)

Report: branch; files created/modified; validations + results; commit message + hash; branch pushed; `main` status + confirmation; final `git status`.
